1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,324
Germany has a reputation for hard work.

2
00:00:04,324 --> 00:00:10,717
Yet the average German worker works fewer hours than anyone else in Europe:

3
00:00:10,717 --> 00:00:11,825
1,335 a year.

4
00:00:12,825 --> 00:00:18,514
A worker in Greece puts in 1,893. That's 558 hours more,

5
00:00:18,514 --> 00:00:22,375
or about fourteen extra working weeks.

6
00:00:23,575 --> 00:00:27,800
So who really works the longest hours in Europe, and does it pay?

7
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:31,181
First, what we're measuring.

8
00:00:31,181 --> 00:00:35,505
These are the hours actually worked in a year by the average worker,

9
00:00:35,505 --> 00:00:38,875
from the Penn World Table, through Our World in Data.

10
00:00:39,875 --> 00:00:44,401
They include overtime, and they include people who work part-time.

11
00:00:44,401 --> 00:00:47,075
That second part matters, as we'll see.

12
00:00:48,075 --> 00:00:53,900
Countries count hours in slightly different ways, so treat small gaps with caution.

13
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:55,725
The big patterns are real.

14
00:00:56,725 --> 00:01:02,150
We're comparing thirty-one countries: the twenty-seven in the EU, plus the UK,

15
00:01:02,150 --> 00:01:04,375
Norway, Switzerland and Iceland.

16
00:01:05,975 --> 00:01:14,787
Here's the ranking for 2023. At the top: Croatia, at 1,974 hours a year.

17
00:01:14,787 --> 00:01:20,050
Then Greece, at 1,893, and Malta, at 1,848.

18
00:01:21,050 --> 00:01:30,425
Cyprus, 1,840. Romania, 1,826. And Poland, 1,807.

19
00:01:31,625 --> 00:01:42,041
Further down, Italy works 1,701 hours a year, Spain 1,638, the UK 1,523,

20
00:01:42,041 --> 00:01:44,500
and France 1,487.

21
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:52,454
And it isn't simply east against west. Czechia works 1,774 hours a year.

22
00:01:52,454 --> 00:01:58,924
But Hungary and Bulgaria work about 1,660, and Estonia about 1,600.

23
00:01:58,924 --> 00:02:01,725
All of them fewer than Italy.

24
00:02:02,725 --> 00:02:13,955
And at the bottom: Sweden, 1,432. Norway, 1,412. Denmark, 1,380. And last of all,

25
00:02:13,955 --> 00:02:16,450
Germany, at 1,335.

26
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:24,052
Spread across the whole year, with holidays and part-time jobs included,

27
00:02:24,052 --> 00:02:28,100
1,335 hours works out at about 26 hours a week.

28
00:02:29,300 --> 00:02:34,885
Just above them sit the Netherlands and Austria, both at about 1,440 hours.

29
00:02:34,885 --> 00:02:40,023
The Netherlands has the highest share of part-time workers in the EU,

30
00:02:40,023 --> 00:02:43,150
and that's a clue to what's going on here.

31
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:49,676
From first place to last, that's a gap of almost 640 hours a year.

32
00:02:49,676 --> 00:02:51,850
About sixteen working weeks.

33
00:02:53,450 --> 00:03:00,132
Now go back to 1970. The average German worker then put in 1,972 hours a year.

34
00:03:00,132 --> 00:03:05,100
That's almost exactly what a worker in Croatia does today.

35
00:03:06,100 --> 00:03:13,225
And of the countries on this chart, Ireland worked the longest: 2,359 hours a year.

36
00:03:14,425 --> 00:03:16,950
Now watch the next five decades.

37
00:03:21,950 --> 00:03:30,734
Then came 2020. Lockdowns and furlough schemes cut hours almost everywhere.

38
00:03:30,734 --> 00:03:38,933
In Britain, the working year fell from 1,545 hours to 1,392. In Italy,

39
00:03:38,933 --> 00:03:41,275
from 1,680 to 1,514.

40
00:03:42,675 --> 00:03:47,434
By 2023, most countries were back close to where they'd been.

41
00:03:47,434 --> 00:03:53,440
And the German working year had shrunk by a third since 1970, to 1,335 hours.

42
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:57,575
That's the biggest fall of any country on this chart.

43
00:03:58,575 --> 00:04:06,462
Ireland came down a long way too, by 30 percent. Greece fell much less,

44
00:04:06,462 --> 00:04:09,350
from 2,114 hours to 1,893.

45
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:15,625
Almost everywhere in Europe, working years got shorter as countries got richer.

46
00:04:17,025 --> 00:04:21,405
Go back further, and the change is even bigger. In 1870,

47
00:04:21,405 --> 00:04:25,160
a German worker put in about 3,300 hours a year.

48
00:04:25,160 --> 00:04:30,400
That's more than sixty hours a week, almost every week of the year.

49
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:38,025
In France it was about 3,200 hours. In the United States, about 3,100. In Britain,

50
00:04:38,025 --> 00:04:43,276
about 2,750. The numbers before 1950 come from a different study,

51
00:04:43,276 --> 00:04:47,639
and they only cover full-time workers outside farming,

52
00:04:47,639 --> 00:04:53,375
so they don't compare exactly with today's. But the direction is clear.

53
00:04:54,375 --> 00:05:01,550
The average German worker today puts in about forty percent of the hours their great-great-grandparents did.

54
00:05:03,150 --> 00:05:07,375
So why is Germany at the bottom? There are three main reasons.

55
00:05:08,575 --> 00:05:10,128
First, part-time work.

56
00:05:10,128 --> 00:05:14,999
Germany has one of the highest shares of part-time workers in Europe,

57
00:05:14,999 --> 00:05:18,600
especially among women. They pull the average down.

58
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:20,806
Second, holidays.

59
00:05:20,806 --> 00:05:26,198
German law guarantees at least twenty days of paid leave on a five-day week.

60
00:05:26,198 --> 00:05:29,675
Many contracts give thirty, plus public holidays.

61
00:05:30,675 --> 00:05:34,085
Third, shorter full-time weeks in many industries,

62
00:05:34,085 --> 00:05:36,950
often agreed between unions and employers.

63
00:05:37,950 --> 00:05:42,966
So it isn't that Germans are lazy. Many of them simply work fewer hours,

64
00:05:42,966 --> 00:05:45,125
and get a lot done in each one.

65
00:05:46,125 --> 00:05:48,641
And that's the other side of the story.

66
00:05:48,641 --> 00:05:54,575
Take everything a country produces in a year and divide it by all the hours its people work.

67
00:05:55,575 --> 00:05:58,450
On the Penn World Table's figures,

68
00:05:58,450 --> 00:06:04,454
an hour of work in Germany produced about 83 dollars of output in 2023,

69
00:06:04,454 --> 00:06:11,134
adjusted for prices. In the United States, about the same. In Poland, about 47.

70
00:06:11,134 --> 00:06:12,825
In Greece, about 38.

71
00:06:13,825 --> 00:06:18,130
So a German hour produces about twice as much as a Greek one.

72
00:06:18,130 --> 00:06:23,281
The OECD's own figures show the same gap. That's mostly down to machines,

73
00:06:23,281 --> 00:06:28,150
technology and how firms are organised, not to how hard anyone tries.

74
00:06:29,750 --> 00:06:35,036
Now look at the top of the ranking again. Croatia, Greece, Romania, Poland.

75
00:06:35,036 --> 00:06:39,688
Mostly countries where wages are lower. When each hour earns less,

76
00:06:39,688 --> 00:06:41,450
people work more of them.

77
00:06:42,450 --> 00:06:46,283
And where more people work for themselves, as in Greece,

78
00:06:46,283 --> 00:06:48,200
hours tend to be longer too.

79
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:55,600
We looked at what those wages really buy in our video on minimum wages across the EU.

80
00:06:57,200 --> 00:07:04,046
For comparison, the average American worker put in 1,789 hours in 2023.

81
00:07:04,046 --> 00:07:10,025
That's more than 450 hours above Germany, and close to Poland.

82
00:07:11,025 --> 00:07:14,124
It wasn't always like that. In 1970,

83
00:07:14,124 --> 00:07:20,838
Germans actually worked more hours than Americans: 1,972 a year against 1,931.

84
00:07:20,838 --> 00:07:26,950
Since then, the American working year has shrunk by just seven percent.

85
00:07:27,950 --> 00:07:33,839
And Japan, famous for long office hours? 1,654 hours a year,

86
00:07:33,839 --> 00:07:40,416
fewer than Poland or Italy. South Korea worked 2,919 hours in 1970,

87
00:07:40,416 --> 00:07:46,600
the most of any country in this data. Today it's down to 1,910.

88
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:55,235
Three things to take away. One: Germany works the fewest hours in Europe,

89
00:07:55,235 --> 00:08:00,150
1,335 a year, while Croatia tops the list at 1,974.

90
00:08:01,150 --> 00:08:05,181
Two: almost everywhere, working years got shorter.

91
00:08:05,181 --> 00:08:08,325
Germany's shrank by a third after 1970.

92
00:08:09,325 --> 00:08:14,830
Three: long hours aren't a sign of a strong economy. The richer a country gets,

93
00:08:14,830 --> 00:08:19,708
the fewer hours its people tend to work. Part of being rich, it seems,

94
00:08:19,708 --> 00:08:21,450
is buying back your time.

95
00:08:22,650 --> 00:08:26,650
Next on Terrachart: what Europeans pay for electricity,

96
00:08:26,650 --> 00:08:29,050
and the winter it all went wrong.

97
00:08:30,250 --> 00:08:32,227
Sources are in the description.

98
00:08:32,227 --> 00:08:37,200
How many hours do you actually work in a normal week? Tell us in the comments.
